\details{
\subsection{Understanding the Results}{

\strong{P-value}: If p < 0.05, the variables are likely related (not independent)
\itemize{
\item p < 0.001: Very strong evidence of relationship
\item p < 0.01: Strong evidence of relationship
\item p < 0.05: Moderate evidence of relationship
\item p >= 0.05: No significant relationship found
}

\strong{Effect Sizes} (How strong is the relationship?):
\itemize{
\item \strong{Cramer's V}: Works for any table size (0 = no relationship, 1 = perfect relationship)
\itemize{
\item < 0.1: Negligible relationship
\item 0.1-0.3: Small relationship
\item 0.3-0.5: Medium relationship
\item 0.5 or higher: Large relationship
}
\item \strong{Phi}: sqrt(chi-squared / N). Reported for every table, as SPSS
does; in a 2x2 table it equals Cramer's V, in larger tables it can
exceed 1 (use Cramer's V there)
\item \strong{Gamma}: For two ordinal variables (-1 to +1, shows the direction of
the relationship). Shown by \code{summary()} only when both variables
are ordered factors or numeric; for nominal variables its sign depends
on the arbitrary category order. \code{goodman_gamma()} always
computes it.
}
}

\subsection{When to Use This}{

Use chi-squared test when:
\itemize{
\item Both variables are categorical (gender, region, education level, etc.)
\item You want to know if they're related or independent
\item You have at least 5 observations in most cells
